# A piece played with both hands: reading a piano score on two staves spaced by length, the left
# hand's finger numbers and bass notes, each hand alone, then both at once and a long note held while
# the other hand moves, with Ode to Joy (Beethoven, 1824) in the lander on the moon.
lesson music-both-hands v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=8 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "A piece with both hands"
  goal "Read a piano piece on two staves and play it with both hands, sounding together the notes that line up."

  look {
    scene 33x37 {
      column score space=0 align=start at=canvas(1, 1) {
        notes right clef=treble notes=[E4, E4, F4, G4, G4, F4, E4, D4] values=[] meter=4 spaced=true
        notes left clef=bass notes=[C3, G2] values=[4, 4] meter=4 spaced=true
      }
      row low space=3 align=end below=score gap=1 {
        hand hands side=both numbers=piano ring=0 colours=false
        lander ship rungs=5
      }
    }
    say """
      In the lander there is a small keyboard, and the crew play Ode to Joy, Beethoven's tune from
      1824. Piano music is written on two staves joined together: the top one, with the treble clef,
      is for the right hand, and the bottom one, with the bass clef, is for the left. Notes written
      one above the other are played at the same moment. Here the left hand holds C for the whole of
      bar 1 while the right hand plays four notes, then moves down to G for bar 2.
      """
  }
  do {
    practice music.hands-together-note count=1 seed=7 easy-seed=7 hard-seed=7
    level easy {
      worked music.hands-lh-finger k=1 level=medium
    }
    practice music.hands-lh-finger count=1 seed=9 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=1
    practice music.hands-play-left count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.hands-play-right count=1 seed=7 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.hands-play-together count=1 seed=7 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    practice music.hands-together-note count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=2 hard-seed=2
    practice music.hands-held-count count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
    level hard {
      show music.hands-claim q=1 r=1
    }
    practice music.moon-hands-count count=1 seed=2 easy-seed=1 hard-seed=7
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.hands-claim q=0 r=0 easy-r=1 hard-r=0
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.hands-perform v=1 easy-v=0 hard-v=2
  }
  remember {
    say "On a piano score, notes one above the other are played together: left to right is time."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The idea is that a piano score is read like a map: left to right is time and top to bottom is
    the two hands, so notes lined up one above the other sound together and a long note in one hand
    is held while the other hand moves. The wrong ideas to watch for are reading the top staff first
    and the bottom one after it, counting the left hand's fingers from the thumb the wrong way,
    since the little finger is 5 on the lowest note, and playing a held note again on the next beat.
    In the working, look for the child's finger running straight down the page before playing. The
    two-star question is the claim that the top staff is played first; the three-star question is a
    performance for you to judge against what it asks, each hand alone first and then together, and
    at medium it asks the child to choose the left hand for bars 3 and 4 by which chord holds more
    of the right hand's notes. A child who is stuck can play each hand alone and then very slowly
    together, saying the beats, and go back to the way in; stay on this lesson another day if the
    hands still take turns. The notes played are judged on the music page; the performance is yours
    to judge in the room. Off the page, play the first line of Ode to Joy together, one of you on
    each hand, then swap.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the notes played together are at the start of a bar, a left-hand finger in C position is worked once in the teacher's pen before another, the left hand to play is C, E, G, B, the notes to play together start bar 1 or bar 2, the held note is the left hand's first, the moments both hands start together are counted under a left hand of C, G, B, the two-star is as written with its answers in another order, and the three-star plays two bars with both hands."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the notes played together are on beat 3, the left hand moves to G position, the left hands to play move between C and G by skips, the notes to play together are on beat 3, the held note is a crotchet B under the right hand's held minim, a claim that a left-hand minim is played again is judged before the two-star, the left hands to compare start a note where the right hand is holding, and the three-star plays the whole line with a left hand in minims and makes up a left hand for the second line."
  }
}
